  "prompt_text": "The figure presents wildfire as a chemical driver that transforms soil and then affects ecosystems and people. In the fire stage, heating causes pyrolysis, volatilization, and combustion, which visibly alter soil minerals and organic matter. The diagram emphasizes dehydration, reduction, oxidation, and condensation reactions, showing that burning can convert chromium from Cr(III) to the more oxidized Cr(VI), while arsenic appears to shift toward As(III) and mercury can volatilize. Fire also leaves pyrogenic organic matter and raises soil alkalinity through ash components.\n\nAfter the fire, soil chemistry continues to change depending on moisture conditions. Under wet conditions, hydration, reduction, and dissolution processes are highlighted, with Cr(VI) tending back toward Cr(III). Under dry conditions, precipitation and oxidation are emphasized, and arsenic appears to be oxidized from As(III) to As(V). These post-fire pathways seem controlled by rainfall versus sun-driven drying.\n\nThe lower landscape panel links these soil reactions to broader consequences. Smoke and dust create human exposure, while burned soils influence soil health, organic matter, revegetation, and downstream water quality. Overall, the image shows fire as a catalyst for metal transformation and long-lasting environmental effects.",
